  "proposed_text": {
    "slide_7_caveat": "Potassium is about 317 mg a serve, 9% of the Thai 3,500 mg reference, or 16% to 21% of the 1,500 to 2,000 mg a day a Thai guideline gives kidney patients on a low-potassium diet. Medicines that raise potassium add risk. Gate 2 requires a medical view.",
    "slide_7_speaker_note": "State the medical caveat aloud. One serve is about 9% of the Thai reference of 3,500 mg a day. A Thai nutrition guideline recommends a low-potassium diet of not more than 1,500 to 2,000 mg a day for chronic kidney disease before dialysis, and its explanation applies that limit when blood potassium is high; for a patient on that limit one serve is about 16% to 21% of the day. KDIGO 2024 sets no daily figure, a secondary review reports the same for KDOQI 2020, and the Thai guideline gives no figure by kidney disease stage. KDIGO 2024 reports about 90% absorption for potassium salts in processed foods, against 50% to 60% for plant foods; applying the 90% to the cube is our extrapolation. Who the caution covers: about 1% of men aged 25 to 34 and about 13% of adults aged 25 and over, about 6 million people, counting kidney disease at stage 3 to 5 and ACE inhibitor or ARB treatment for high blood pressure only (model bounds 0.1% to 5.5% and 5% to 25%; derived). The bounds are our model bounds, not a statistical interval: the central value is a midpoint of assumed bounds, and the width comes mainly from the kidney input (the survey chosen and, for adults, the split of people aged 60 and over), the share of young adults who are treated, the factor for young men's high blood pressure, the ACE inhibitor and ARB share (taken from a hospital sample with mean age 65) and the overlap rule. About three quarters of the adult figure is people aged 60 and over. A reviewer may reject the serve, require the precaution, or ask for less carbonation: potassium falls by about 58 mg for each 0.1 volume of carbon dioxide removed (derived).",
    "stated_limit": "Potassium and kidney disease are a stated limit of this proposal. One serve contains about 317 mg of potassium, about 9% of the Thai reference intake of 3,500 mg a day. A Thai nutrition guideline recommends a low-potassium diet of not more than 1,500 to 2,000 mg a day for adults with chronic kidney disease before dialysis, and its explanation applies that limit when blood potassium is high. For a patient on that limit one serve is about 16% to 21% of the day. KDIGO 2024 sets no daily figure, a secondary review reports the same for KDOQI 2020, the Thai guideline gives no figure by kidney disease stage, and the UK Kidney Association (2023) describes the historical low-potassium diet as 2 to 3 g a day and does not recommend a blanket restriction. KDIGO 2024 reports absorption of about 90% for potassium salts added to processed foods. We apply that figure to the dissolved cube as our own extrapolation, and the cube has not been tested for absorption. US medicine labels and the UK Kidney Association guideline name potassium supplements and salt substitutes as adding to the risk of high blood potassium for people taking ACE inhibitors, ARBs and similar medicines. Our model, built from Thai survey data, puts the share of men aged 25 to 34 who have kidney disease at stage 3 to 5 or take an ACE inhibitor or ARB for high blood pressure at about 1%, and the share of adults aged 25 and over at about 13%. The model bounds are 0.1% to 5.5% and 5% to 25%. They are the extremes of our assumptions, not a statistical interval, and the central values are midpoints of those assumptions. Other potassium-raising medicines and heart failure are not counted, because no Thai figure was found. The kidney count includes people who do not know they have kidney disease (in Thai SEEK only 1.9% of people with kidney disease of any stage were aware of it), so a warning that begins with a diagnosis reaches few of them. No clinician has reviewed the serve. The proposal therefore carries a precaution, makes no potassium claim, and holds sale until a medical review is complete.",
    "slide_7_source_line": "Thai reference: Notification of the Ministry of Public Health No. 445 B.E. 2566, Annex 3, English translation published by the Thai FDA (primary). Kidney guidance: KDIGO 2024 (primary); UK Kidney Association, Management of Hyperkalaemia in Adults, October 2023 (primary); KDOQI 2020 as reported in secondary reviews (Molina et al., Frontiers in Medicine 2021; Lambert et al., Nephrology 2022); Clinical Practice Recommendation for Nutritional Management in Adult Kidney Patients, B.E. 2561 (updated B.E. 2563), Thai Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition with the Nephrology Society of Thailand, recommendation 4.4.3 (primary). Medicine labels on DailyMed (primary): Cozaar, Aldactone, Inspra, amiloride, Zestril. Food values: USDA FoodData Central (primary). Coverage: NHES V, NHES III, NHES VI, Thai SEEK and National Health Security Office prescribing data (primary; derived, with proxies named)."
  },
